    {
      "question_id": "Q06",
      "question": "Why does the Transformer use multiple attention heads instead of a single head?",
      "expected_answerable": true,
      "status": "answered",
      "answer": "The Transformer uses multiple attention heads so the model can jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions—something averaging in a single head inhibits [S2]. Multi‑head attention also counteracts the reduced effective resolution caused by averaging attention‑weighted positions [S4], while keeping total computational cost similar to a single full‑dimension head by using smaller per‑head dimensions (dk = dv = dmodel/h) [S1]. Empirically, single‑head attention performed worse (about 0.9 BLEU lower) than the best multi‑head setting, though too many heads can also hurt performance [S5].",
